A dictionary dw has a string (which represents the title of a document) as a key and a list of strings (which represent words in the document) as the corresponding value. Define a function compute_word_frequency(dw) that is given dw, and returns a dictionary that maps each word $w$ to the frequency (the number of occurrences) of $w$ over all the lists in dw.
For example, if dw is
{'t1': ['I', 'have', 'book'], 't2': ['I', 'student'], 't3': ['I', 'go', 'book', 'store']},
then, compute_word_frequency(dw) should return
{'I': 3, 'have': 1, 'book': 2, 'student': 1, 'go': 1, 'store': 1}.
...
def compute_word_frequency(dw):
    a = list(dw.values())
    b = {}
    for i in range(len(a)):
        for j in range(len(a[i])):
            if a[i][j] in b:
                b[a[i][j]] += 1
            else:
                b[a[i][j]] = 1
    return b

assert compute_word_frequency({'t1': ['I', 'have', 'book'], 't2': ['I', 'student'], 't3': ['I', 'go', 'book', 'store']}) == {'I': 3, 'have': 1, 'book': 2, 'student': 1, 'go': 1, 'store': 1}
